      "body_plain": "Hi all,\n\nI found and fixed the problem. I had used `worldMap@polygons[[x]]@Polygons[[1]]@coords` , but `worldMap@polygons[[x]]@Polygons` can be a list of multiple elements, with 1 being the mainland and 2, 3, ... being islands or other areas that are not connected to the mainland. That is why there were several gaps on my original map.\n\nThe corrected code and the resulting plots are here: https://github.com/brechtdv/ggplot2-EU-map\n\nCheers,\nBrecht\n\nOp maandag 11 mei 2015 12:37:21 UTC-4 schreef Andy South:\n\n> Thanks JB for your great answers to this and the other question.\n> I'm [finally] on the case addressing the issues you have raised before. Expect more news this week.\n> All the best,\n> Andy\n>\n> On 10 May 2015 at 22:38, Jean-Baka Domelevo Entfellner <jean...@sanbi.ac.za> wrote:\n> > It's probably an issue with the underlying map imported from Natural\n> > Earth (http://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/10m-cultural-vectors/10m-admin-0-countries/).\n> > The borders are not compliant with the views of the UN General\n> > Assembly, I already mentioned this to Andy in the past. See th\n> > disclaimer by the people who compile Natural Earth: \"Natural Earth\n> > Vector draws boundaries of countries according to defacto status. We\n> > show who actually controls the situation on the ground. Please feel\n> > free to mashup our disputed area themes to match your particular\n> > political outlook.\"\n> >\n> > Natural Earth is \"supported by NACIS (North American Cartographic\n> > Information Society)\". Personally I suspect that their maps often\n> > reflect the viewpoint of (some people in) the United States :-( Hence\n> > the fact they \"forgot\" Kaliningrad...\n> >\n> > Regards,\n> >    JB\n> >\n> > On Sun, May 10, 2015 at 10:07 PM,  <brec...@gmail.com> wrote:\n> > > I am trying to use ggplot2 to make nicer rworldmap plots, based on the code\n> > > I found here: http://editerna.free.fr/wp/?p=130\n> > >\n> > > The plot generated by the code below shows that Kaliningrad (Russian part in\n> > > Europe) and Thrace (European Turkey) are missing. Perhaps because of the\n> > > discontinuity with the main polygon? Is there any way to make these parts\n> > > reappear?\n> > >\n> > > On a related note, I noticed that worldMap$NAME[207] is NA.\n> > >\n> > >\n> > > ## load packages\n> > > library(ggplot2)\n> > > library(rworldmap)\n> > >\n> > > ## identify countries\n> > > worldMap <- getMap()\n> > > show <- which(worldMap$NAME %in% c(\"Russia\", \"Turkey\", \"Greece\"))\n> > >\n> > > ## convert to data.frame\n> > > showCoords <-\n> > >   lapply(show,\n> > >          function(x) {\n> > >            df <- data.frame(worldMap@polygons[[x]]@Polygons[[1]]@coords)\n> > >            df$region <- as.character(worldMap$NAME[x])\n> > >            colnames(df) <- list(\"long\", \"lat\", \"region\")\n> > >            return(df)\n> > >          })\n> > > showCoords <- do.call(\"rbind\", showCoords)\n> > >\n> > > ## gplot\n> > > ggplot() +\n> > >   geom_polygon(\n> > >     data = showCoords,\n> > >     aes(x = long, y = lat, group = region))\n> > >\n> > > --\n> > > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\n> > > \"rworldmap\" group.\n> > > To post to this group, send email to rwor...@googlegroups.com.\n> > > Visit this group at http://groups.google.com/group/rworldmap.\n> > > To view this discussion on the web, visit\n> > > https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/rworldmap/e5ede547-7b37-4834-8bf1-2b50e466b611%40googlegroups.com.\n> >\n> > --\n> > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups \"rworldmap\" group.\n> > To post to this group, send an email to rwor...@googlegroups.com.\n> > Visit this group at http://groups.google.com/group/rworldmap.\n> > To view this discussion on the web, visit https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/rworldmap/CAFTWPdJ67zg6eC%3Dnir%3DywaK6DCYP-TZYGovbRj1g4GvwAPsj2Q%40mail.gmail.com.\n>",
